Array.prototype.sort: compare numbers directly for an a - b comparator

A comparator that is exactly function (a, b) { return a - b; } or (a, b) => a - b, or the same with b - a, is
recognized by its bytecode, as JavaScriptCore and SpiderMonkey recognize it. When every value to sort is a number,
the values are sorted by a stable merge sort on the numbers instead of a call of the comparator per comparison.
The result is the same: without NaN the comparison is a total order with stable ties, which has one result; with
NaN, which compares equal to everything, the previous algorithm is kept with a direct comparison. Such a function
has no side effects on numbers, so skipping its calls is not observable.

